  "body": "The one case left from the third review of PR #223 (#208): https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/pull/223#issuecomment-6715273489. **Not new**: the base and main do the same.\n\nA ball r30, UV-meshed at 120×60 or finer (≤ 3° facets), with a 10×10×100 square rod along Z through its centre (two bodies, one STL), reads a **trusted** X or Y axis with nothing set aside. A 6×6×80 rod and a 40×4×100 flat bar do the same, and a rotated copy reads the rotated cross axis. Turning about X would sweep the 100 mm rod into a disc. Coarser balls (72×36 and below) are refused since #223.\n\nCause: the ball's facets all meet at its centre, so they share a group with the rod's faces. Their latitudes run continuously, giving one 'wide' class that #223's per-class ring check skips. The rod's faces show no latitude pattern by bearing, and the ball supplies every bearing.\n\nDone means: inside a wide latitude class, bin by latitude and require each bin's area to be spread evenly over the bearings, or take flat faces out of a wide class and judge them on their own. Pin: the 120×60 ball plus square rod is refused or reads Z, never X or Y. Every #208 and #223 pin stays green.",
